There's something you need to realize about nethack.
Stuff is *scarce*.  

You're lucky to find *anything* useful.  Shops don't get more inventory, 
and what you see is what you get.

Another thing you need to realize, is that apart from basic survival,
pretty much the whole point of the first half of the game (or so) is
identifying these items whose names have been deliberately obscured.

I have developed quite a definite strategy for identifying items while 
consuming a minumum number of the items, and also while attempting to
maximize the yeild of the items I do use.  Pretty much the last thing 
I'd do is read a scroll, unless I have a very good idea that it's a
blessed scroll of identify.  In fact, unless I have an emergency, I
would usually prefer to save an item until I can maximize its
usefulness, than to use it up trying to figure out what it is.  I find
this approach makes no discrenable difference in the survivability of 
a character (although it is nice to get early armor enchantments, and 
I'm not too proud to use an early genocide).

I hardly even *touch* scrolls before I've gotten my character to at
least level 7.  And when I do start touching scrolls, I usually have
some information about them:

1.  I know which one is scare monster.
2.  I know the relative prices of them, from chatting with shopkeepers.
3.  I often know which one is teleport, having seen a monster use one.

Long before this, I usually know something about 5 or more wands, and 
also about some rings.  I also have a strategy for identifying potions 
via deduction.  

Pretty much the reason I want scrolls of identify is to identify the 
other scrolls, identify the jewelry, and to know how many charges are 
left on the wands and chargeable tools.

But basically, my game is more focused on preserving these items,
protecting them from damage, and consolidating them in containers at a
convenient location.  There's usually a pretty quick series of events
where I go from knowing almost nothing about the items, to just about
knowing what everything is.  That's usually the start of what I call
"the middle game".

Now, spellcasters are going to want to know books and scrolls much
earlier than fighters.  Obviously you have to adjust your strategy 
depending on your class and your situation.  


